      "worked": "The solutions are the intersections of $y=ax-x^2$ with $y=9$ and $y=-9$.\n$$ax-x^2=-\\left(x-\\frac a2\\right)^2+\\frac{a^2}{4}.$$\nThe downward-opening parabola has maximum $\\tfrac{a^2}{4}\\ge0$, so $y=-9$ always gives two distinct intersections. Exactly three solutions require $y=9$ to touch the vertex once.\n$$\\begin{align*}\\frac{a^2}{4}&=9\\\\a^2&=36\\\\a&=\\pm6\\end{align*}$$\nFor either value, $ax-x^2=9$ has the single root $x=\\tfrac a2$, while $ax-x^2=-9$ has the two roots $x=\\tfrac a2\\pm3\\sqrt2$. These three roots are distinct.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
